## BACKGROUND ON IMPACT AND REACH

IMPACT Initiatives is a humanitarian NGO, based in Geneva, Switzerland. The organisation manages several initiatives, including the REACH Initiative. The IMPACT team comprises specialists in data collection, management and analysis, GIS and remote sensing. IMPACT was launched at the initiative of Acted, an international NGO with headquarters based in Paris and presence in thirty countries. The two organizations have a strong complementarity formalized in a global partnership, which allows IMPACT to benefit from Acted’s operational support in its fields of intervention.

### We are currently looking for a Data Specialist to support our team in Syria, based in Damascus.

**Department**: REACH

**Position**: Data Specialist

**Contract duration**: 9 months (extension dependent on funding)

**Starting Date**: 1st December 2026

**Location**: Damascus, Syria.

## COUNTRY PROFILE

- After more than a decade of conflict, economic collapse, and large-scale displacement, a regime change in December 2024 marked a new phase in Syria’s history. Conditions remain volatile, marked by regular armed clashes, prolonged socioeconomic and infrastructure challenges, and limited access to basic services such as healthcare, education, water, and electricity. While it is widely acknowledged that Syria needs large investments in development in order to recover from years of destruction and economic degradation, the humanitarian response also continues at scale due to high levels of need after fifteen years of conflict, exacerbated by the economic situation and poor basic services infrastructure.
- Active in Syria since 2013, the IMPACT Syria mission has been producing key information products over the last decade to provide an evidence base for an informed humanitarian response. Activities have ranged from monthly humanitarian situation overviews and market monitoring, to displacement tracking, to supporting joint planning processes through the provision of multi-sectoral needs data, to providing evidence for durable solutions via area-based assessments.

## FUNCTIONS

The REACH Data Specialist will fulfill the following functions:

Under the line management of the IMPACT Senior Research Manager, the Data Specialist ensures the validity and strengthens the quality and efficiency of all IMPACT research cycles in Syria, in accordance with IMPACT’s standards, partner expectations, and IMPACT’s ethos of facilitating an evidence-based response. In coordination with assessment, data, and GIS teams, the Data Specialist will lead in the provision of technical inputs and outputs at all stages of relevant research cycles, including for secondary data reviews, assessment conceptualization, research design, data cleaning, data analysis, production of information products (inclusive of maps and factsheets), and dissemination of information products, etc. S/he may be involved in partner coordination, donor reporting, presentations, and input to the strategic development of IMPACT in Syria. The Data Specialist will also be responsible for developing and implementing a continuous capacity building program for data team members, and could lead on providing trainings to partners if relevant.

In his/her mission, the Data Specialist will be hosted by IMPACT’s global partner Acted and will fall under the direct responsibility and management of Acted’s Country Director and his/her delegates for all Administrative, Security, and relevant Logistics and Finance issues. S/he will therefore fully abide to Acted’s Security, Administration, and relevant HR, Logistics and Finance rules and regulations.

## RESPONSIBILITIES

The Data Specialist responsibilities include the following:

***RESEARCH PLANNING AND IMPLEMENTATION***

- In coordination with the Assessment teams, ensure that assessments are planned in line with project objectives and with IMPACT’s guidelines;
- For each assessment, ensure contribution to and review of draft ToRs with a specific focus on the sampling design, data collection methodology, analysis plan, as well as data management plan,
- In coordination with Assessment team, co-construct qualitative and quantitative data collection tools, ensuring data requirements of research cycle/assessment are met;
- Develop data processes and scripts as is relevant, ensure that support is provided by data staff in overseeing data collection, including validation as part of daily assessment checks;

***DATA MANAGEMENT, CLEANING AND ANALYSIS***

- Ensure that all collected data is stored in line with IMPACT’s Data Management Guidelines, and with the research project ToRs (Data Management Plan Annex);
- Ensure that data is cleaned, all revisions are recorded, and datasets are maintained and securely stored;
- Ensure data analysis conducted on collected data as per ToRs, and is validated by IMPACT HQ before output product drafting stage;
- Ensure the quality and accuracy of technical information provided as well as the confidentiality and protection of collected information;
- Perform additional analyses on external datasets and internal previous datasets as relevant/required;

***TEAM MANAGEMENT***

- Direct management of data team, including line management of data officers and assistants, including recruitment and staff career management, and the development of work plans and performance indicators;
- Collaborate closely with Country Representative, Senior Research Manager and Associate Research Managers, in terms of work planning and technical guidance of the relevant data staff;
- Conduct regular meetings with all data team members to assess progress in all research cycles and to review work plan;
- Reviewing data staff work throughout research cycles (cleaning datasets, analysis scripts, maps, etc.)
- Be available to provide regular support and technical backstopping.

***EXTERNAL ENGAGEMENT***

- In coordination with the IMPACT Country Representative, engage in relevant technical fora in country.
- Support the presentation of IMPACT data products to relevant third parties, to enhance their use and impact.

***STRATEGY DEVELOPMENT AND IMPLEMENTATION***

- Under the guidance of the Country Representative, contribute to the development and implementation of IMPACT’s strategy in Syria.
- Work with clusters and partner organizations and working groups to understand the various relevant data gaps within Syria and identify how IMPACT will fill these gaps;
- Engagement with HQ on research and/or coordination and provide support to relevant IMPACT Global Community of Practices (CoPs) (as relevant).

The Data Specialist will maintain the strictest confidentiality on all data collected and related processes. He/she will actively take measures to prevent the unauthorized sharing of any information and data belonging to IMPACT and its partners, or collected during his/her assignment with IMPACT.

## REQUIREMENTS

- **Academic** Excellent academic qualifications, preferably including a Master’s degree in relevant discipline (e.g. Statistics, Data Science, Data Analytics);
- **Research skills** Excellent research and analytical skills required, including a sound understanding of sampling methods and their application, an ability to clean and analyse large data sets, as well as conduct temporal or spatial trend analysis. Demonstrable experience of data science. Excellent data analysis skills. Experience with GIS is desirable.
- **Software skills** Advanced knowledge of R or Python is required. Proven record of writing clean, reusable code with an emphasis on functional programming. Emphasis on thinking about system design and building data processes that scale and repeat with minimal maintenance. Experience with GitHub preferred. Strong knowledge of PowerBi or R Shiny is highly desirable. Proven knowledge of Microsoft Office including Word, Excel, and PowerPoint is required. Knowledge of ArcGIS Pro/ QGIS/ Leaflet an advantage. If available, please share a link to a GitHub or similar repository you have worked on as part of your application.
- **Management skills** Demonstrable experience of line managing and building the capacity of technical staff.
- **Familiarity aid system** Familiarity with the aid system and with the research community;
- **Years of work experience** At least 4 years of relevant working experience
- **Training skills** Ability to conduct trainings and mentor IMPACT teams and partners on data/ assessment skills;
- **Communication/reporting skills** Excellent communication and drafting skills for effective reporting;
- **Multi-tasking skills** Experience multitasking with numerous ongoing projects and ability to prioritise;
- **Level of independence** Strong existing ability to work independently in support of colleagues and partners;
- **Cross-cultural work environment** Ability to operate in a cross-cultural environment requiring flexibility;
- **Experience in geographical region** Past experience in Syria or the Middle East region is desirable;
- **Security environment** Ability to operate in a complex and challenging security environment;
- **Language skills** Fluency in English required, competency in Arabic is a benefit;

## COMPENSATION & BENEFITS

- For this position, salary between 3’420 CHF and 3’480 CHF monthly (before income tax), etc as well as a monthly living allowance of 300 USD *NB - IMPACT salaries are strictly determined by our salary grid depending on the grade of the position and the level of education of staff. A location-dependent security and/or isolation adjustment is then applied as a recognition that some staff are required to work in difficult places where living and working conditions are much more difficult than elsewhere.*
- Accommodation and food provided in the guesthouse.
- Enrolment in Swiss private pension fund (Swisslife – approx. 9.975% of staff gross salary), health insurance, life insurance and repatriation assistance.
- Flight tickets every 6 months & visa fees covered (in-country travel costs and professional expenses are fully covered).
- R&R every 3 months (flight ticket up to 500$ + 200$ of living allowance).
- Contribution to the luggage transportation: between 20 and 100 kgs, depending on the length of the contract (+ luggage and personal property insurance)
- Annual leave of 36 days per year. Public holidays of the country of assignment. Family/compassionate leave when applicable.
- Predeparture induction - 3 days at IMPACT Initiatives’s HQ in Geneva + one week pre-departure training in Acted HQ in Paris, including a 4-days in situ security training;
- IMPACT prioritizes the psychological safety of its staff and the health insurance provided covers, among others, up to 1000 € per year of psychosocial counselling fees.

**Contract Type:** Individual Consultancy; Project-Based  
**Eligibility:** **Syrian nationals or non-Syrian consultants currently residing in Syria only**

Trust Consultancy & Development (TCD) is seeking experienced consultants to join its roster of experts in **Gender Equality, Disability Inclusion and Social Inclusion (GEDSI)** to support upcoming evaluations, assessments, research, Third-Party Monitoring (TPM), programme design and advisory assignments in Syria.

Selected consultants will be included in TCD's consultant roster and may be considered for future short-term consultancy opportunities based on their technical expertise, geographical experience, availability and the requirements of specific assignments.

**Primary geographic scope:** Syria

## About the Call

Trust is expanding its roster of experts with strong technical and practical experience in **gender equality, disability inclusion, social inclusion, intersectionality and inclusive humanitarian and development programming**.

We are particularly interested in experts with **recent, first-hand knowledge of Syria's social, economic and institutional context**, including the barriers experienced by women and girls, persons with disabilities, older persons, displaced and returning populations, youth and other groups facing exclusion or unequal access to services, livelihoods, participation and decision-making.

Experts with experience integrating GEDSI considerations into **evaluations, assessments, research, programme design, monitoring and Third-Party Monitoring** are particularly encouraged to apply.

## Areas of Expertise

Applicants should have strong experience in one or more of the following:

1. **Gender Equality and Women's Empowerment**, including gender-responsive programming, women's economic and social empowerment, participation, leadership and access to opportunities and services;
2. **Gender Analysis**, including analysis of gender roles, inequalities, power relations, access to and control over resources, decision-making and barriers affecting women and girls;
3. **Disability Inclusion**, including disability-inclusive programming, accessibility, reasonable accommodation, participation of persons with disabilities and removal of physical, communication, institutional and attitudinal barriers;
4. **Social Inclusion**, including identification and analysis of social, economic, geographic and institutional barriers affecting marginalized and underserved groups;
5. **Intersectionality**, including analysis of how gender, disability, age, displacement, socioeconomic status, location and other factors interact to shape vulnerability, exclusion, access and opportunities;
6. **Gender, Age and Disability Mainstreaming**, including integration of inclusive approaches across programme design, implementation, monitoring, evaluation and learning;
7. **Women's Economic Empowerment**, including livelihoods, employment, entrepreneurship, market access, financial inclusion, skills development and women's participation in economic activities;
8. **Disability-Inclusive Livelihoods and Economic Inclusion**, including access to employment, vocational training, entrepreneurship, social protection and livelihood opportunities for persons with disabilities;
9. **Inclusive Education and Skills Development**, including barriers to education, TVET, life skills and employment pathways for girls, young women, persons with disabilities and other groups facing exclusion;
10. **Inclusive Social Protection**, including equitable targeting, accessibility, cash and social assistance, economic inclusion and identification of barriers affecting access to social protection programmes;
11. **Inclusive Health and Basic Services**, including equitable access to health, rehabilitation, education, WASH and other essential services for women, persons with disabilities and marginalized populations;
12. **Participation, Voice and Decision-Making**, including meaningful participation of women, youth, persons with disabilities and marginalized groups in programme design, community structures and decision-making processes;
13. **Organizations of Persons with Disabilities (OPDs) and Civil Society Engagement**, including participatory approaches, organizational capacity development, localization and engagement with representative organizations and community-based groups;
14. **Accessibility and Reasonable Accommodation**, including physical accessibility, accessible communication, inclusive information systems and identification of environmental and institutional barriers;
15. **Gender- and Disability-Responsive Data**, including sex-, age- and disability-disaggregated data, disability measurement approaches, inclusive data collection and analysis of inequalities and exclusion;
16. **Inclusive and Participatory Research**, including accessible research methods, participatory assessments, consultations with marginalized populations and ethical approaches to engaging groups experiencing exclusion;
17. **Safeguarding and Do-No-Harm**, including safe and inclusive participation, informed consent, confidentiality, prevention of exploitation and abuse and identification of risks associated with programme participation;
18. **Institutional GEDSI Mainstreaming**, including organizational assessments, policies, strategies, action plans, capacity building and development of practical tools for mainstreaming gender equality, disability and social inclusion;
19. **GEDSI Research, Monitoring and Evaluation**, including gender and inclusion assessments, baselines/endlines, evaluations, outcome studies, barrier analyses, accessibility assessments, mixed-method research and Third-Party Monitoring;
20. **GEDSI Capacity Development**, including development and delivery of training, coaching, technical guidance and tools for government institutions, NGOs, civil society organizations and programme teams.

## Qualifications & Experience

Applicants should have:

- **Syrian nationality and current residence in Syria, or be a non-Syrian consultant currently based in Syria**;
- A relevant university degree in gender studies, disability studies, sociology, social sciences, development studies, human rights, social work, public policy, economics or another relevant field;
- At least **5 years of relevant professional experience**; senior/lead experts should preferably have **10+ years**;
- Recent professional experience related to **gender equality, disability inclusion and/or social inclusion in Syria**, preferably within the last three years;
- Demonstrated experience conducting or contributing to **GEDSI assessments, evaluations, research, programme design, monitoring, TPM or technical advisory assignments**;
- Strong understanding of Syria's **social and institutional context and the barriers affecting women and girls, persons with disabilities and other groups experiencing exclusion**;
- Experience engaging with women and girls, persons with disabilities, OPDs, youth, displaced and returning populations, communities, civil society organizations, authorities, NGOs, UN agencies and other relevant stakeholders;
- Demonstrated understanding of **participatory, inclusive, accessible and do-no-harm approaches**;
- Experience applying **gender-, age- and disability-sensitive research and data-collection methods** is strongly preferred;
- Strong research, analytical, facilitation and report-writing skills;
- Arabic fluency; professional English is strongly preferred.

## International Organization & Donor Experience

Previous experience with **UN Women, UNICEF, UNFPA, UNDP, UNHCR, ILO, IOM, WHO, World Bank, UN agencies, EU/ECHO-funded programmes, GIZ, international NGOs or other organizations working on gender equality, disability inclusion and social inclusion** is a strong advantage.

Experience with relevant international approaches and frameworks, including **Gender Equality and Social Inclusion (GESI/GEDSI), disability-inclusive development, gender mainstreaming, intersectionality, the UN Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (CRPD), the Washington Group Questions, Age, Gender and Diversity (AGD) approaches and inclusive humanitarian action**, should be clearly highlighted.

Applicants should indicate relevant assignments, including the **client, assignment, year, role and main responsibilities**.

**Contract Type:** Individual Consultancy; Project-Based  
**Eligibility:** **Syrian nationals or non-Syrian consultants currently residing in Syria only**

Trust Consultancy & Development (TCD) is seeking experienced consultants to join its roster of experts in **Protection, Gender-Based Violence (GBV), Child Protection (CP) and related protection programming** to support upcoming evaluations, assessments, research, Third-Party Monitoring (TPM), programme design and advisory assignments in Syria.

Selected consultants will be included in TCD's consultant roster and may be considered for future short-term consultancy opportunities based on their technical expertise, geographical experience, availability and the requirements of specific assignments.

**Primary geographic scope:** Syria

## About the Call

Trust is expanding its roster of experts with strong technical and practical experience in **protection, GBV, child protection, protection monitoring, case management, safeguarding and community-based protection**.

We are particularly interested in experts with **recent, first-hand knowledge of Syria's protection context**, including protection risks affecting conflict-affected, displaced and returning populations; women and girls; children and adolescents; persons with disabilities; older persons; and other groups facing heightened protection risks or barriers to accessing services.

Experts with experience in **protection assessments, evaluations, research, programme quality, monitoring and Third-Party Monitoring** are particularly encouraged to apply.

## Areas of Expertise

Applicants should have strong experience in one or more of the following:

1. **General Protection**, including protection analysis, protection monitoring, identification of protection risks, vulnerability analysis, referrals and protection-sensitive programming;
2. **Gender-Based Violence (GBV)**, including GBV prevention, risk mitigation, response services, survivor-centred approaches, referral pathways and community-based interventions;
3. **Child Protection (CP)**, including prevention and response to violence, abuse, exploitation and neglect, child protection case management, family strengthening and community-based child protection;
4. **Protection Case Management**, including identification, assessment, case planning, referrals, follow-up, case closure, supervision and quality assurance;
5. **GBV Case Management and Referral Systems**, including safe and confidential referral pathways, survivor-centred service provision and coordination among relevant service providers;
6. **Child Protection Case Management**, including best-interest procedures, referral mechanisms, family tracing/reunification where relevant, alternative care and case-management quality;
7. **Protection Monitoring and Analysis**, including monitoring protection risks and trends, community consultations, protection assessments, service mapping and evidence-based protection analysis;
8. **Community-Based Protection**, including community protection structures, community engagement, awareness raising, outreach and strengthening community capacities to identify and address protection risks;
9. **Protection of Women and Girls**, including safe spaces, women's and girls' empowerment, GBV risk mitigation, access to services and participation in community decision-making;
10. **Protection of Children and Adolescents**, including child-friendly approaches, psychosocial wellbeing, violence prevention, positive parenting, safe spaces and adolescent participation;
11. **Protection of Persons with Disabilities and Older Persons**, including disability inclusion, accessibility, age-sensitive programming and identification of barriers to services and participation;
12. **Gender, Inclusion and Protection Mainstreaming**, including gender analysis, age, gender and diversity approaches, disability inclusion and integration of protection principles across humanitarian and development programmes;
13. **Safeguarding, PSEA and Child Safeguarding**, including prevention of sexual exploitation and abuse, safeguarding systems, codes of conduct, complaints and reporting mechanisms, risk assessments and organizational capacity strengthening;
14. **Housing, Land and Property (HLP) and Civil Documentation**, including documentation-related protection barriers, tenure issues, displacement and return-related HLP risks and access to relevant services;
15. **Mine Action-Related Protection and Explosive Ordnance Risk Education**, particularly where protection programming intersects with risks affecting children, displaced populations and returning communities;
16. **Protection and MHPSS Linkages**, including psychosocial support, safe referrals, community-based support and integrated approaches for individuals affected by violence, displacement and other protection risks;
17. **Protection in Cash and Assistance Programmes**, including protection risk analysis, safe and inclusive targeting, protection mainstreaming and identification of risks associated with humanitarian assistance;
18. **Protection Coordination and Institutional Strengthening**, including coordination mechanisms, referral systems, service mapping, capacity development, localization and engagement with authorities and protection actors;
19. **Protection Research, Monitoring and Evaluation**, including protection assessments, baselines/endlines, evaluations, outcome monitoring, perception surveys, qualitative research, mixed-method studies and Third-Party Monitoring;
20. **Accountability to Affected Populations (AAP)**, including participation, feedback and complaints mechanisms, information provision, community engagement and safe and accessible accountability systems.

## Qualifications & Experience

Applicants should have:

- **Syrian nationality and current residence in Syria, or be a non-Syrian consultant currently based in Syria**;
- A relevant university degree in social sciences, psychology, social work, law, human rights, gender studies, development studies, international relations, public health or another relevant field;
- At least **5 years of relevant professional experience**; senior/lead experts should preferably have **10+ years**;
- Recent professional experience related to **Protection, GBV and/or Child Protection in Syria**, preferably within the last three years;
- Demonstrated experience conducting or contributing to **protection assessments, evaluations, research, programme design, monitoring, TPM or technical advisory assignments**;
- Strong understanding of Syria's **protection context, displacement and return dynamics, vulnerable populations, service-delivery environment and key protection risks and barriers**;
- Experience engaging with communities, protection service providers, local organizations, authorities, NGOs, UN agencies and other relevant stakeholders;
- For GBV specialists, demonstrated understanding of **survivor-centred approaches, confidentiality, safe referrals, GBV risk mitigation and ethical principles for GBV-related data collection**;
- For Child Protection specialists, demonstrated understanding of **child safeguarding, best interests of the child, child-sensitive research and ethical engagement with children and caregivers**;
- Strong understanding of **confidentiality, informed consent, do-no-harm principles and safe data collection when working with vulnerable populations**;
- Strong research, analytical, facilitation and report-writing skills;
- Arabic fluency; professional English is strongly preferred.

## International Organization & Donor Experience

Previous experience with **UNHCR, UNICEF, UNFPA, UN Women, IOM, UN agencies, EU/ECHO-funded programmes, international NGOs, Red Cross/Red Crescent Movement actors or other organizations working on protection, GBV and Child Protection** is a strong advantage.

Experience with relevant international approaches and standards, including **Protection Principles, GBV Minimum Standards, Child Protection Minimum Standards (CPMS), IASC Guidelines, PSEA, safeguarding, protection mainstreaming, survivor-centred approaches and community-based protection**, should be clearly highlighted.

Applicants should indicate relevant assignments, including the **client, assignment, year, role and main responsibilities**.

**Contract Type:** Individual Consultancy; Project-Based  
**Eligibility:** **Syrian nationals or non-Syrian consultants currently residing in Syria only**

Trust Consultancy & Development (TCD) is seeking experienced consultants to join its roster of experts in **Health, Public Health, Mental Health and Psychosocial Support (MHPSS), health systems and community-based health services** to support upcoming evaluations, assessments, research, Third-Party Monitoring (TPM), programme design and advisory assignments in Syria.

Selected consultants will be included in TCD's consultant roster and may be considered for future short-term consultancy opportunities based on their technical expertise, geographical experience, availability and the requirements of specific assignments.

**Primary geographic scope:** Syria

## About the Call

Trust is expanding its roster of experts with strong technical and practical experience in **health, public health, primary healthcare, mental health, psychosocial support, community health and health-system strengthening**.

We are particularly interested in experts with **recent, first-hand knowledge of Syria's health and MHPSS context**, including access to essential health services, health-system capacity, needs of conflict-affected and displaced populations, mental health and psychosocial needs, health workforce challenges, and approaches to strengthening inclusive, accessible and resilient health services.

## Areas of Expertise

Applicants should have strong experience in one or more of the following:

1. **Public health and primary healthcare (PHC)**, including essential health services, community health, preventive care, health promotion and integrated service delivery;
2. **Mental Health and Psychosocial Support (MHPSS)**, including community-based MHPSS, psychological first aid, individual and group interventions, psychosocial activities and integration of MHPSS into health and other humanitarian services;
3. **Mental health services**, including clinical and non-clinical interventions, referral pathways, case management, community mental health and integration of mental health into primary healthcare;
4. **Health systems strengthening**, including health governance, service delivery, health workforce, health information systems, referral systems, quality of care and institutional capacity development;
5. **Maternal, newborn, child and adolescent health (MNCAH)**, including reproductive, maternal and newborn health, child health, adolescent health and integrated service delivery;
6. **Sexual and reproductive health and rights (SRHR)**, including reproductive health services, family planning, maternal health and access to appropriate and inclusive services;
7. **Communicable and non-communicable diseases**, including prevention, surveillance, case management, continuity of care and community-based approaches;
8. **Emergency and humanitarian health**, including health responses in conflict, displacement and emergency settings, emergency preparedness, outbreak response and continuity of essential services;
9. **Community-based health and outreach**, including community health workers, mobile teams, health awareness, risk communication and community engagement;
10. **MHPSS for children, adolescents and youth**, including psychosocial wellbeing, resilience, social-emotional support, caregiver support, safe spaces and school/community-based interventions;
11. **Protection-sensitive and inclusive health/MHPSS services**, including services for persons with disabilities, older persons, women and girls, displaced populations, returnees and other groups facing barriers to accessing services;
12. **Health workforce development**, including training, supervision, capacity building, staff wellbeing, supportive supervision and competency development;
13. **Quality of care and patient-centred services**, including service standards, patient experience, accountability, safeguarding, confidentiality and feedback mechanisms;
14. **Health and MHPSS research, monitoring and evaluation**, including needs assessments, baselines/endlines, evaluations, health facility assessments, service mapping, outcome measurement, mixed-method research and Third-Party Monitoring;
15. **Health information and data**, including health information systems, epidemiological analysis, health indicators, surveillance, data quality and evidence-based planning;
16. **Health policy, coordination and institutional strengthening**, including health planning, coordination mechanisms, localization, capacity development and engagement with health authorities and humanitarian/development actors.

## Qualifications & Experience

Applicants should have:

- **Syrian nationality and current residence in Syria, or be a non-Syrian consultant currently based in Syria**;
- A relevant university degree in medicine, public health, psychology, psychiatry, nursing, social work, health sciences, epidemiology, health management or another relevant field;
- At least **5 years of relevant professional experience**; senior/lead experts should preferably have **10+ years**;
- Recent professional experience related to **health and/or MHPSS in Syria**, preferably within the last three years;
- Demonstrated experience conducting or contributing to **health/MHPSS assessments, evaluations, research, programme design, TPM or technical advisory assignments**;
- Strong understanding of Syria's **health context, health system, service-delivery challenges, population health needs and barriers to accessing health and MHPSS services**;
- Experience engaging with health authorities, health facilities, healthcare workers, community-based organizations, affected communities, NGOs, UN agencies and other relevant stakeholders;
- For MHPSS specialists, demonstrated understanding of **MHPSS frameworks, referral pathways, community-based approaches and appropriate ethical and safeguarding considerations**;
- Strong research, analytical, facilitation and report-writing skills;
- Arabic fluency; professional English is strongly preferred.

## International Organization & Donor Experience

Previous experience with **WHO, UNICEF, UNFPA, UNHCR, IOM, World Bank, UN agencies, EU/ECHO-funded programmes, international NGOs, Red Cross/Red Crescent Movement actors or other organizations working on health and MHPSS** is a strong advantage.

Experience with relevant international approaches and standards, including **WHO health-system frameworks, IASC MHPSS Guidelines, humanitarian health standards, community-based MHPSS, primary healthcare, health-system strengthening and quality-of-care approaches**, should be clearly highlighted.

Applicants should indicate relevant assignments, including the **client, assignment, year, role and main responsibilities**.

## BACKGROUND ON IMPACT & REACH

REACH was born in 2010 as a joint initiative of two International NGOs ([IMPACT Initiatives](http://www.impact-initiatives.org/) and [ACTED](http://www.acted.org/)) and the United Nations Operational Satellite Applications Programme ([UNOSAT](http://www.unitar.org/unosat/)). REACH’s **purpose** is to promote and facilitate the development of information products that enhance the humanitarian community’s decision making and planning capacity for emergency, reconstruction and development contexts. REACH facilitates information management for aid actors through three complementary services: (a) need and situation assessments facilitated by REACH teams; (b) situation analysis using satellite imagery; (c) provision of related database and (web)-mapping facilities and expertise.

IMPACT Initiatives is a humanitarian NGO, based in Geneva, Switzerland. The organization manages several initiatives, including the REACH Initiative. The IMPACT team comprises specialists in data collection, management and analysis and GIS.

**We are currently looking for a (Senior) Research Officer to support our team in Nigeria.**

**Department**: REACH

**Position**: (Senior) Research Officer

**Contract duration**: 6 months

**Location**: Dakar, Senegal, with possible travel to Abuja, Nigeria

**Starting Date**: October 2026.

## COUNTRY PROFILE

Nigeria is among the world's most critical hunger hotspots, where protracted conflict, displacement, economic pressures, and climate shocks are driving rapidly worsening humanitarian needs. In the Northeast, more than 8.8 million people require urgent assistance, including 4.9 million children, sustained displacement, protection risks, severe funding shortfalls, a growing nutrition emergency, and a cholera outbreak. Food insecurity is expected to reach record levels, with an estimated 34.7 million people nationwide projected to face Crisis-level food insecurity or worse by August 2026. Meanwhile, the Northwest faces a different but equally severe crisis driven by chronic poverty, banditry, communal violence, and recurrent flooding, which have displaced hundreds of thousands of people (800,000 IOM DTM R18), damaged livelihoods and farmland, and contributed to sharp declines in agricultural production. Together, these overlapping shocks are undermining resilience, increasing humanitarian needs, and threatening both current food security and future harvests.

## FUNCTIONS

Under the line management of the Research Manager, the (Senior) Research Officer is responsible for the management and implementation of IMPACT’s research cycles in country, including the preparation, data collection, analysis, drafting, dissemination, and evaluation stages. Throughout research cycles, the (Senior) Research Officer engages with partners to promote their participation and maximize their impact. He/she also ensures an efficient and transparent use of resources required for project implementation. This can include the direct supervision of assessment teams.

In his/her mission, the (Senior) Research Officer will be hosted by IMPACT or it’s partner in Nigeria- FACT foundation and will fall under the direct responsibility and management of the IMPACT Country Representative and FACT Executive Director and his/her delegates for all Administrative, Security, and relevant Logistics and Finance issues. S/he will therefore fully abide by IMPACT/FACT Foundation’s Security, Administration, and relevant HR, Logistics, and Finance rules and regulations.

In her/his mission, the (Senior) Research Officer will support the implementation of thematic assessments related to but not limited to Cash Based Programming and varied Multi-Sector Needs Assessment outputs. This research will seek to provide timely thematic and multi/-sectoral research outputs on differentiated humanitarian needs and response/program functionality and efficacy. It aims primarily to inform emergency humanitarian planning at the strategic, programmatic, and operational levels, particularly where other data sources are limited or unavailable. This project will require frequent external engagement with programmatic partners and key stakeholders within the humanitarian response in Nigeria, and internal coordination with all units operating within the Nigeria team.

## RESPONSIBILITIES

The (Senior) Research Officer is responsible for overseeing the planning, implementation and follow up of research cycles to which s/he is assigned, in close coordination with other team members and with relevant external partners. In doing so, the (Senior) Research Officer will ensure an effective management of related projects and resources, including (when relevant) line-management national and international assessment staff.

More specifically the (Senior) Research Officer is responsible for the following:

Research Planning:

***Research Planning***

- Anticipate knowledge gaps prior to the execution of a Research Cycle;
- Ensure that required secondary data review and/or analysis has been conducted in preparation of an assessment, with a particular focus on gender, protection, and accountability to affected populations (AAP);
- Design research approaches and methodologies according to IMPACT’s requirements and principles as well as partners’ information needs, ensuring gender sensitivity and inclusivity.
- Compose and construct, in close coordination with GIS and data teams, qualitative and quantitative data collection tools that integrate gender-disaggregated and participatory approaches.
- For each research cycle, prepare ToRs and ensure their validation by HQ before any data collection begins.
- Ensure relevant stakeholders and partners (especially gender and protection actors) are engaged in research design and planning.

***Research Implementation***

- In close liaison with field officer(s), ensure that required enumerators are identified and trained for primary data collection;  
    In close liaison with field officer (s), Monitor data collection, ensuring its correct implementation in line with agreed TORs;  
    In close liaison with field officer (s), Manage assessment logistics, including with partner organizations, identifying assessment areas, facilitating design workshops, managing joint data collection processes, and facilitating joint analysis workshops;
- In close liaison with field officer (s), Ensure regular situation updates on data collection are produced and circulated to line manager, relevant colleagues and external counterparts. Provide support and follow up on identified challenges during the data collection process;
- Ensure that the line manager and IMPACT HQ research department are alerted to any issues that prevents full implementation of the methodology agreed in the approved TORs. Ensure that all changes to the methodology are documented throughout implementation, and that any change is formally validated by IMPACT HQ;
- Keep track of progress and delays of all assigned assessments throughout the research cycle. Ensure that delays or identified challenges for specific assessments are reported in writing and orally in a timely manner;  
    In close liaison with field officer (s), Ensure logistics, financial, administration, security and HR processes directly related to ongoing and upcoming assessments are appropriately planned, implemented and coordinated with the relevant ACTED departments;  
